\section{This is not the published metric}{

Beverly et al. define exposure isotropically and Khan et al. validated it
isotropically. Directional weighting is a \strong{departure}, it has no validation
of its own, and both concentration parameters are judgements of the analysis
rather than properties of the data — exactly like the weights of
\code{\link[=fev_vuln_stack]{fev_vuln_stack()}}. They are written into the provenance for that reason. Set
both to zero and you get the published metric back, which is the default and
the thing to compare against.
}

\section{How the two weights are built}{

Each sector \code{k} has a mean bearing. Its weight is the product of two von
Mises-like factors, \code{exp(kappa * cos(delta))}, normalised over sectors so
they sum to one:
\itemize{
\item \strong{Wind.} \code{delta} is the angle between the sector bearing and the direction
the wind comes \strong{from} — fire arrives from upwind, so that is the
direction that must weigh more. \code{kappa_wind} is constant over the map.
\item \strong{Slope.} \code{delta} is the angle between the sector bearing and the
direction of steepest descent at the assessed cell, which is what
\code{terra::terrain(v = "aspect")} returns. Fire runs uphill, so a cell is
exposed from below. \code{kappa} here is \strong{not} constant: it is
\code{kappa_slope * tan(slope)}, so flat ground gets no anisotropy at all and
steep ground gets a lot. That is the behaviour you want and it falls out
of the physics rather than being imposed.
}
}

\section{What it costs}{

\code{n_sectors} focal passes, each over roughly \code{1 / n_sectors} of the ring, so
the weighted-operation count is about the same as the isotropic version. What
is not the same is memory: \code{n_sectors} intermediate layers are held at once.
Eight sectors on a departmental grid is the point where \code{filename} stops
being optional.

Eight is the default because 45 degrees is about the angular precision a
wind direction deserves; more sectors buy resolution the input does not have.
}

\section{On wind direction}{

\code{wind} is a \strong{meteorological} direction in degrees: the direction the wind
blows \emph{from}, clockwise from north, as \code{\link[=fev_fetch_weather]{fev_fetch_weather()}} returns it and
as every anemometer reports it. A north wind is 0 and pushes fire southwards,
so the exposure it raises is on the northern side of a cell. Passing a vector
direction here — the way the wind goes — inverts the whole result, and there
is no way for the function to detect it.
}

